.TH NC 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
nc \- copy data to and from a TCP connection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nc
.I host
.RI [ port ]
.br
.B nc \-l
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RI [ addr ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nc
connects its standard input and standard output to a TCP connection.
Without
.BR \-l ,
it connects to
.I host
at
.IR port .
With
.BR \-l ,
it waits for one connection to arrive.
Either way, it sends what it reads from standard input to the connection,
and writes what it reads from the connection to standard output.
.PP
It speaks TCP over IPv4 and IPv6, and nothing else.
There is no UDP, and no way to run a program on the connection.
.SS Connecting
.I host
is a name, a dotted IPv4 address, or an IPv6 address.
Windows resolves a name.
When it has several addresses,
.B nc
tries only one: the first IPv4 address, or else the first address.
.PP
.I port
is a number from 0 to 65535, or a service name; see
.BR NOTES .
A port may instead follow the host after a colon, as in
.BR example.com:80 ,
and then it overrides the
.I port
operand.
This port must be a number.
An IPv6 address with more than one colon is never split this way, so give
it a port in brackets, as in
.BR [::1]:80 .
With no port at all,
.B nc
tries port 0, and the connection fails.
.SS Listening
With
.BR \-l ,
.B nc
binds a socket, accepts the first connection from any peer, and closes
the listening socket.
It prints nothing when the connection arrives.
.PP
.I addr
is the local address to bind.
It must be numeric: a name is an error.
Without it,
.B nc
listens on all addresses, IPv4 and IPv6 alike.
With an IPv4 address it accepts IPv4 connections only, and with an IPv6
address, IPv6 only.
To accept only connections from the local machine, give
.B 127.0.0.1
or
.BR ::1 .
As with a host, a port may follow the address, as in
.B 127.0.0.1:8000
or
.BR [::1]:8000 ,
and overrides
.BR \-p .
.SS Relaying
Once connected,
.B nc
waits until standard input or the connection has data, reads up to 1024
bytes, and writes them to the other side before it reads again.
Data to and from files and pipes is not translated; for consoles, see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
When standard input reaches the end of the file,
.B nc
shuts down the sending direction of the connection, so the peer sees end
of file, and keeps copying what the peer sends.
A failed read from standard input is treated the same way, without a
message.
.PP
When a read from the connection returns end of file or fails,
.B nc
exits with status 0.
This happens as soon as the peer closes the connection or shuts down its
sending direction, even if standard input has more data.
That data is lost.
Whatever the peer sent before is written first.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-lp ,
and the argument of
.B \-p
may be attached or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options, and so does the first operand: an option after it is
taken as an operand.
.TP
.B \-l
Listen for one connection instead of connecting.
The number of times
.B \-l
is given is the backlog of the listening socket.
With
.BR \-l ,
at most one operand is allowed, and without it, one or two.
.TP
.BI "\-p " port
With
.BR \-l ,
listen on local port
.IR port ,
a number from 0 to 65535 or a service name.
The default is 0, which has Windows choose a free port.
.B nc
does not report which.
Without
.BR \-l ,
.B \-p
is accepted and ignored: the local port of an outgoing connection is
always chosen by Windows.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.PP
Any other option, a missing option argument, or the wrong number of
operands prints the usage on standard error and exits with status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The connection ended or a read from it failed, or the usage was
requested with
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line, a host or port that could not be understood, a
socket that could not be created, bound, or listened on, a connection
that could not be made or accepted, or an error writing to the
connection or to standard output.
The message is on standard error, prefixed with
.BR nc: .
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before all output was written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Chat across the loopback interface.
In one window, listen:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ nc \-l \-p 8000 127.0.0.1
.EE
.RE
.PP
In another, connect.
Each line typed in either window appears in the other:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ nc 127.0.0.1 8000
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy a file to another machine.
On the receiver, at a console, listen and write to a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ nc \-l \-p 9000 >data.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
On the sender, send the file.
Both commands exit when it has been sent:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ nc 192.168.1.5 9000 <data.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Answer one HTTP request with a fixed page.
The request is written to the terminal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqHTTP/1.0 200 OK\er\en\er\enhi\en\(aq | nc \-l \-p 8000 127.0.0.1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Connect to an IPv6 address, giving the port with it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ nc \(aq[::1]:8000\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
There is no timeout option.
Stop after 10 seconds with
.BR timeout :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ timeout 10 nc 192.168.1.5 9000 <data.bin
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from other versions of netcat:
.IP \(bu 2
The only options are
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.BR \-\-help .
There are none for UDP, timeouts, scanning, verbose output, a source
address, or running a program.
.IP \(bu 2
Options must come before the operands.
.IP \(bu 2
In listen mode the operand is a local address, not a port.
.B nc \-l 8000
takes
.B 8000
as an address and fails with
.BR "nc: bind" .
Give the port with
.BR \-p .
.IP \(bu 2
Nothing is printed when a connection is made or accepted.
.PP
A service name is looked up in
.IR /etc/services ,
which busybox takes to be
.I \eetc\eservices
in the root of the current drive.
Windows does not normally have such a file, so use port numbers.
.PP
Socket failures are reported without the Windows reason, as in
.B nc: bind
and
.BR "nc: can't connect to remote host (127.0.0.1)" .
The address is shown only for IPv4.
A failed write to the connection, as when the peer has gone, prints
.BR "nc: write error: Invalid argument" ,
a misleading reason.
.PP
The listening socket sets
.BR SO_REUSEADDR ,
which on Windows lets sockets share a port.
A second
.B nc
can listen on a port that another
.B nc
is listening on without an error, and Windows decides which of them
receives a connection.
.PP
The sending direction of the connection is shut down as soon as standard
input ends.
When standard input is already at end of file, as with
.BR </dev/null ,
this happens at once, and the
.B nc
at the other end then exits, which can cut off data still being sent to
it.
Run a receiving
.B nc
at a console, or give it input that does not end.
.PP
Standard input and output are binary, so bytes such as CR LF, Ctrl-Z, and
NUL pass through files and pipes unchanged.
A console delivers a line to
.B nc
when you press Enter, with a CR LF line ending.
.B nc
reads the console a line at a time, so data from the peer is not shown
while you are typing a line.
To end the input, type Ctrl-Z at the start of a line and press Enter.
.PP
When standard output is a console, received text is converted for display
from the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later.
Escape sequences, such as those for color, take effect, and busybox
emulates them on consoles without virtual terminal support.
A block of data that contains a NUL byte is written unconverted.
.PP
There is no connection timeout other than the one Windows applies.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR timeout (1),
.BR wget (1)
